        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Antisemitism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1879 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val">Wilhelm Marr, who coined Antisemitismus and founded the Antisemiten-Liga in 1879; Moritz Steinschneider, who used the word antisemitische in 1860 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a>; Joseph Arthur de Gobineau, a racial theorist; Heinrich von Treitschke, who coined “the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are our misfortune”; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>; Louis de Bonald; Édouard Drumont; Adolf Stoecker; Leon Pinsker, who called it Judeophobia</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Wagner’s Das Judenthum in der Musik (1850); Gobineau’s Essay on the Inequality of the Human Race (1853–1855); Marr’s Der Sieg des Judenthums über das Germanenthum (1879); Luther’s On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and their Lies (1543); Bonald’s Sur les juifs (1806); Gougenot des Mousseaux’s Le Juif, le judaïsme et la judaïsation des peuples chrétiens (1869); The Protocols of the Elders of Zion, a forgery published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1903</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> form a distinct and inferior race; that Jews are an alien collective within host nations; that Jews control finance and seek world power through a secret conspiracy; that Jews corrupt a nation’s culture; that Jews are collectively guilty of killing Christ; that Jews are responsible for political and economic crises</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">antisemitism in far-right politics and violence, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> denial, in conspiracy theories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the Arab world, and a rise in anti-Jewish hate crimes after October 2023; scholars dispute the concept of a “new antisemitism” and the definitions in use</span></div>
