    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The term Antisemitismus was first printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1879 as a scientific-sounding word for Judenhass, but anti-Jewish sentiment is traced to the 3rd century BCE, and scholars debate whether earlier hostility belongs under the same name.</li><li>Religious antisemitism, rooted in the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, was joined in the 19th century by racial antisemitism, which held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were a distinct and inferior race and that baptism did not change this.</li><li>Racial antisemitism culminated in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where eleven million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were targeted for extermination and some six million were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word antisemitic was first used in 1860 by Moritz Steinschneider to characterise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a>’s ideas about “Semitic races”; Wilhelm Marr’s league of 1879 made it the name of a political cause.</p></aside>
    </section>

