    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">167 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a> erects an altar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> in the Temple and bans circumcision, Shabbat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> study, sparking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabean-revolt">Maccabean Revolt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1096</span><span class="rf-text">Hundreds or thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are killed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, the first major outbreak of anti-Jewish violence in Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1348</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are blamed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>; 900 are burned alive in Strasbourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pope Clement VI</a> issues two bulls to protect them</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1543</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> writes On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and their Lies, recommending a pogrom against them</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1879</span><span class="rf-text">Wilhelm Marr publishes The Victory of the Jewish Spirit over the Germanic Spirit and founds the Antisemiten-Liga</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1935</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-laws">Nuremberg Laws</a> forbid marriages between Aryans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and strip German Jews of full citizenship</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938</span><span class="rf-text">The Kristallnacht pogrom of 9–10 November kills at least 91 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and burns or damages about 1,400 synagogues</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1942</span><span class="rf-text">The mass murder by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einsatzgruppen">Einsatzgruppen</a> that began in 1941 culminates in the systematic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes.jpg" alt="Antiochus IV Epiphanes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antiochus_IV_Epiphanes_-_Altes_Museum_-_Berlin_-_Germany_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Many authors see the roots of modern antisemitism in both pagan antiquity and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The first clear examples of anti-Jewish sentiment can be traced to the 3rd century BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, where the Egyptian priest and historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> wrote scathingly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a> issued one of the earliest anti-Jewish edicts, in about 170–167 BCE, which sparked the revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a>. Edward Flannery describes ancient antisemitism as essentially cultural, a national xenophobia played out in political settings, while Bohak has argued that early animosity is not antisemitic unless it was held against the Jews alone, since many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> showed animosity toward any group they regarded as barbarians.</p><p>As Christianity became the dominant religion, increasingly restrictive legislation and rhetoric was directed against Jews. Religious antisemitism, also called anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, is antipathy towards Jews because of their perceived religious beliefs, typically with doctrines of supersession that expect Jews to turn away from Judaism.</p><p>The word itself is newer. Moritz Steinschneider used antisemitische Vorurteile in 1860 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a>, and in 1879 the German journalist Wilhelm Marr used Antisemitismus in a popular pamphlet, accusing the Jews of a worldwide conspiracy against non-Jews. In the same year he founded the Antisemiten-Liga, the first German organisation committed specifically to combating the alleged threat the Jews posed to German culture and advocating their forced removal. The word entered English from German in 1881.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-ford"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-ford.jpg" alt="Henry Ford" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-ford">Henry Ford</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_ford_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fred Hartsook, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Antisemitism is not a single doctrine, and writers have offered several taxonomies of it. Bernard Lazare, writing in the 1890s, named Christian, economic and ethnologic antisemitism; William Brustein names religious, racial, economic and political forms; Louis Harap adds social, ideological and cultural ones. Dietz Bering writes that, to antisemites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are totally bad by nature and their traits incorrigible, so that Jews are to be seen as a collective, remain alien in the societies around them, and secretly bring disaster on their host societies or on the world.</p><p>Racial antisemitism held that Jews are a distinct and inferior race compared to their host nations. It gained mainstream acceptance in the late 19th and early 20th centuries as part of the eugenics movement, drew on the racial theory of Joseph Arthur de Gobineau and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism</a>, and claimed the superiority of northern European “Aryans” over “Semitic” Jews. Its holders saw Jews as beyond redemption even if they converted, whereas religious antisemitism in theory ended with conversion. The race theories behind it were pseudoscientific.</p><p>Economic antisemitism rests on the claim that Jews perform harmful economic activities, above all that they control the world’s finances; the forged Protocols of the Elders of Zion promoted this, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ford">Henry Ford</a> repeated it. Political antisemitism, in William Brustein’s definition, is hostility based on the belief that Jews seek national or world power. Cultural antisemitism, in Louis Harap’s, charges the Jews with corrupting a culture and supplanting it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="Martin Luther" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d.%C3%84._-_Martin_Luther,_1528_(Veste_Coburg).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the Visigothic kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> issued anti-Jewish edicts from the late 6th century. Under Islamic rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were classed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a> and could practise more freely than in Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but pogroms took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1011 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1066, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, who had taken control of the Maghrib and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> by 1147, treated dhimmis harshly. In Christian Europe Jews were persecuted with blood libels, expulsions, forced conversions and massacres, with a first peak during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>. Jews were banished from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1290, 100,000 were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1394, and in 1348 they were made scapegoats for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>’s pamphlet of 1543 is called by Paul Johnson the first work of modern antisemitism.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> confined Jews to the Pale of Settlement in 1772, and the May Laws of 1882 followed the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dreyfus-affair">Dreyfus Affair</a> in France began in 1894, and Karl Lueger in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and Adolf Stoecker in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> used antisemitism in politics. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> antisemitism reached its peak in the interwar period; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ford">Henry Ford</a>’s The Dearborn Independent ran from 1919 to 1927. Since the early 20th century incidents rose sharply across the Arab world, largely through Arab conspiracy theories influenced by European ones.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> came to power in 1933, the German government instituted repressive legislation that denied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> basic civil rights. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-laws">Nuremberg Laws</a> of September 1935 prohibited marriages and sexual relations between “Aryans” and Jews and stripped all German Jews of full citizenship. In the state-sponsored pogrom of 9–10 November 1938, Kristallnacht, at least 91 Jews were killed, thousands were arrested, over 7,500 Jewish businesses were destroyed and about 1,400 synagogues were burned or damaged. After war began in 1939, antisemitic legislation and propaganda were extended to German-occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, often adapting local traditions, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Jews were forced into ghettos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, Lvov, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a> and Radom.</p><p>After the attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1941, mass murder by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einsatzgruppen">Einsatzgruppen</a> culminated from 1942 to 1945 in systematic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. Eleven million Jews were targeted for extermination by the Nazis, and some six million were eventually killed.</p><p>Other states also practised it. Close to 50,000 Jews were killed in pogroms during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. In the Soviet Union a campaign against “rootless cosmopolitans”, a euphemism for Jews, began after 1948, and Yiddish-language poets, writers and artists were killed or arrested; the fabricated Doctors’ Plot of 1952 led to the arrest and execution of Jewish professionals, and the Slánský show trial of the same year alleged an international Zionist conspiracy. After the Kielce pogrom, Polish Jewish survivors fled postwar Poland.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt.jpg" alt="Hannah Arendt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt">Hannah Arendt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannah_Arendt_auf_dem_1._Kulturkritikerkongress,_Barbara_Niggl_Radloff,_FM-2019-1-5-9-16_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Barbara Niggl Radloff, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Scholars dispute both the definition and the history. The eternalist view sees antisemitism as separate from other prejudice, a transhistorical force culminating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt">Hannah Arendt</a> criticised it for answering “Why the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of all people?” with “Eternal hostility”. The contextual view treats it as a type of racism tied to its historical setting, and David Engel argues that the term implies links between anti-Jewish prejudices of different contexts without evidence of them.</p><p>Its causes are explained in competing ways. Theodor Adorno wrote that “Anti-Semitism is the rumour about the Jews”. Bernard Lazare’s 1894 book argued that Jewish separateness helped cause hostility, a belief he later abandoned. Julie Mell’s scholarship finds Jews were not overrepresented in medieval moneylending and that the greed stereotype projected Christian taboos on the minority. David Nirenberg argues antisemitism is embedded in Western thought and adapts to each period. Some scholars read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>’s “On the Jewish Question” as antisemitic; Francis Wheen and David McLellan argue it was written as a defence of the Jews against Bruno Bauer.</p><p>The 2016 IHRA working definition is among the most controversial documents on the subject, with critics arguing it has been used to censor criticism of Israel; the Nexus Document and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> Declaration followed in 2021. Proponents of a “new antisemitism” argue that anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a> can serve as a vehicle for attacks on Jews, while critics say the concept silences debate on Israel.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 1945, and especially once the full extent of the Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> was known, the word antisemitism took on a pejorative sense; Yehuda Bauer wrote in 1984 that nobody now says “I am anti-Semitic”, whereas before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was not uncommon for a person or a party to call itself antisemitic. Style guides, the Associated Press from 2021 among them, now favour the unhyphenated spelling, in part because, as Emil Fackenheim argued in 2003, it avoids implying an entity called “Semitism”.</p><p>Antisemitism persists in contemporary far-right politics and has been a factor for perpetrators of violence such as the Pittsburgh and Poway synagogue shooters, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> denial and conspiracy theories are counted among its forms. Attacks on Jewish memorials, synagogues and cemeteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> rose after the economic crisis of 2008, and a significant increase in anti-Jewish hate crimes worldwide after the Hamas-led attack on Israel of 7 October 2023. A July 2026 report by the J7 task force found more than 20 people killed in antisemitic incidents in seven countries in 2025.</p><p>Education is a stated remedy, though Dara Horn argues that teaching it through the Holocaust is unhelpful and proposes curiosity about Jewish life instead. The study of antisemitism remains politically contested because of differing interpretations of the Holocaust and of the Israeli–Palestinian conflict.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Antisemitism is hostility to or discrimination against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, rooted in ancient prejudice and Christian anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and named in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1879. Its racial form culminated in the Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of some six million Jews, and scholars still dispute how to define it.</em></p>

